  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y=2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(6x+{30}^{\u2218})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=6x+{30}^{\u2218}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of \n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061X.\n$$\n\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=n\\cdot {180}^{\u2218}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =n\\cdot {180}^{\u2218} \\\\ 6x+{30}^{\u2218} & =n\\cdot {180}^{\u2218} \\\\ 6x & =-{30}^{\u2218}+n\\cdot {180}^{\u2218} \\\\ x & =-{5}^{\u2218}+n\\cdot {30}^{\u2218}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=-{5}^{\u2218}+n\\cdot {30}^{\u2218},$ where $n$ is any integer.",
